  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-06",
    "title": "A Farewell to Swift",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  It is time to say goodbye to  NASA 's  Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory . After nearly 22 years of service, the satellite  re-entered Earth's atmosphere  on November 4 at 7:39 EST over the  North Sumatra  region of  Indonesia . The  featured image  is a mosaic created in 2018 using the first one million  images  captured by the satellite's  Ultraviolet/Optical Telescope . Swift had a combination of instruments, including an  X-ray telescope  and a  gamma-ray telescope , that worked together to enable humanity to study, in unprecedented detail, the fastest and most energetic  explosions  in the universe. The most recent  solar maximum  accelerated the decay of the satellite's orbit. An  innovative mission  to  rescue Swift  by boosting its orbit was  not successful . Swift burned on  reentry  like a shooting star, but the impressive data acquired by the telescope over its mission will continue to bring light to the mysteries of the cosmos for years to come. May it keep inspiring new missions  to boldly go  into space, explore new data and seek out new science.     Tomorrow's picture:  what's next?",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Swift_Millionth_Image_Mosaic.jpg?w=5252&h=3744&fit=clip&crop=faces,focalpoint",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-6-a-farewell-to-swift/",
    "credit": " NASA / Swift  and  AndreaMosaic ",
    "alt": "A mosaic image of the Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory, created from one million images of astronomical objects captured by the satellite’s Ultraviolet/Optical Telescope.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-06T08:54:48.343719+00:00"
